## 1. Scope

This reference covers dual wall, adhesive lined heat shrink tubing with a 2:1 recovery ratio. It is aimed at the harness and connector engineer who has to specify both a mechanical covering and a water seal with one part.

## 2. Construction

- Cross-linked polyolefin outer wall providing the mechanical and dielectric covering.
- Inner melt layer of hot melt adhesive that flows during recovery and fills the space around the joint.
- The adhesive layer is the seal; the outer wall is the insulation. They are specified separately even though they arrive as one tube.
- Supplied expanded at 2:1, in discrete lengths or on a reel for automatic cutting.

## 3. Typical specification range

| Parameter | Typical value | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shrink ratio | 2:1 | Enough for a straight splice or connector body, not for a large irregular shape. |
| Continuous operating temperature | -55 C to +125 C (typical polyolefin) | Confirm against the specific grade datasheet. |
| Recovery | Hot air, typically 120 to 150 C air temperature | Adhesive flow begins before the wall reaches full recovery. |
| Flame behaviour | UL 224 VW-1 (common specification) | Flame rating is a property of the grade, not of the ratio. |

Values above describe what is commonly specified for this component class. Confirm the exact figure against the datasheet for the grade being purchased before design release.

## 4. Selection and sizing

- The largest diameter the assembly has to pass over, since 2:1 limits how irregular the joint can be.
- Whether the joint needs a true water seal or only a covering, which decides whether the adhesive layer is required at all.
- The stiffness acceptable after recovery, because the adhesive layer changes the feel of a flexible harness.
- Recovery temperature window against the materials in the assembly, so the adhesive flows before anything nearby is damaged.
- Whether the part is cut by hand or on an automatic machine, which changes the tolerance on the supplied length.

## 5. Standards commonly specified

- UL 224, extruded insulating tubing.
- SAE-AMS-DTL-23053 and the adhesive lined polyolefin slash sheet within that family.
- IEC 60684 series, flexible insulating sleeving.
- ASTM D2671, test methods for heat shrinkable tubing.

Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Recover from the centre outwards, or from one end in a continuous sweep, so trapped air is pushed ahead of the adhesive rather than sealed in.
- Do not overheat to make the tube recover faster. Scorching and an adhesive that has been burnt both reduce sealing performance.
- Clean the substrate before recovery. Adhesive bonds to what it touches, including contamination.
- Keep the assembly constrained until the part has cooled, since the adhesive is still soft while hot.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Water still reaches the conductor | Adhesive flow insufficient, or a channel left along a wire bundle | Size the tube so the adhesive has to fill the gap, recover evenly, and check for unfilled channels on a sample. |
| Adhesive overflow onto the connector | Tube started too far from the centre or too much adhesive in the grade chosen | Centre the tube and confirm the grade selection against the joint volume. |
| Uneven recovery, flat spots | Heat applied from one side only or the tube not fully recovered before cooling | Heat around the circumference and continue until the wall has visibly recovered. |
| Cold cracking in service | Low temperature grade mismatch on an outdoor or under-hood harness | Select the temperature grade for the coldest expected service condition. |

## 8. Typical applications

- Automotive and appliance harnesses where a splice has to be sealed against water.
- Connector to cable interfaces on sensors and sealed assemblies.
- General electrical insulation where both a covering and a seal are needed in one step.
- Distribution and field repair where a repair has to match the original harness build quality.

